WEBVTT
NOTE Cedwyn film, frame 011, en. Timings estimated from the script.

1
00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:16.077
<v the parish clerk entering the Llandough >John Williams. Farm. Owner: Lord Bute. About seventy-six acres. Gross: forty-one pounds, and nought shillings. The same as the last rate.

2
00:00:16.427 --> 00:00:26.042
<v the Bute estate agent>Correct. Now Lord Bute himself: woods in hand, thirty-two acres, three roods, and thirty-nine perches. Then Daniel Thomas and Son: a lime kiln and quarry.

3
00:00:26.392 --> 00:00:44.854
<v Gwen>While you're at it: the new directory, Worrall's, off the carrier's cart. Llandough parish, Penarth section: 'Williams John, Great house.' In print for all Cardiff. And Slater's had him there in sixty-eight too. But how does John hold the place, tell me? On a lease? Year to year?

4
00:00:45.204 --> 00:00:53.281
<v the parish clerk entering the Llandough >This book shows whom the overseers of the poor charge, Gwen, and for what. No lease, no terms. That is all.

5
00:00:53.631 --> 00:01:07.092
<v the Bute estate agent>How a man holds his land is the estate's business, not the parish's. And the estate's business this year is the Llandough limeworks. The plan is drawn on the deed at the office in Cardiff.
